WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.166
- I'm all right, everybody,

00:00:01.166 --> 00:00:02.530
we're gonna get to my first guest.

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:04.690
And she is ready now, so
let's get her in here.

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.940
She was one of the most
iconic artists of the 90s,

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:08.827
when she was called the,

00:00:08.827 --> 00:00:11.950
"Queen of Alt-Rock
Angst" by Rolling Stone.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.790
She sold more than 75
million albums worldwide.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:18.470
Her latest is called, Such
Pretty Forks in the Road.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:20.819
It's out right now, I love it.

00:00:20.819 --> 00:00:24.060
Let's say hi to Alanis
Morissette, everybody!

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:24.893
- Hi!

00:00:24.893 --> 00:00:27.850
- I'm literally such a big fan
that they were talking to me

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:32.350
about the design for our
backstage for this show.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.962
And I wish that you were really
at our show because (laughs)

00:00:34.962 --> 00:00:36.810
I would have put you in your room,

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.663
because you and Annie
Lennox are my first two.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.780
She's one room in you're one room.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.210
There's several different dressing rooms

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.100
and I'd have their vinyl
of the person on the door

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.500
and it's called that room.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.170
And you are one of my
of my rooms, because--

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.067
- [Alanis] I'm so happy,
I'm one of your rooms.

00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:54.140
- [Kelly] (laughs) You're one of my rooms.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.540
- What happens in those rooms though?

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.373
That's what I wanna know (laughs).

00:00:56.373 --> 00:00:57.206
- It's so awesome.

00:00:57.206 --> 00:00:58.500
It's a family show, it's not that bad.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.134
- Please make me proud.

00:01:00.134 --> 00:01:02.420
- (chuckles) There's not as cool

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:03.450
rock and roll stuff happening,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.410
but anyway I have the, Jagged Little Pill.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:08.410
But honestly, I know everybody

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:09.620
talks about Jagged Little Pill,

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.443
but I feel sometimes
you're that writer that,

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.650
literally what people are thinking

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.133
or having a hard time
with or struggling with,

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.680
it's like you put it,
we can't make the words,

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.949
we can't get it out and you do it for us.

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:26.580
Some of us we write songs, we have fans

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.900
and da da da, you're on an epic scale.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.690
Is that a lot of pressure
when you release a record

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.710
and you're so vulnerable at it

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.260
and so raw and people are questioning,

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.380
oh, who's this about, what's this about?

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.000
Or, you know, what are you going through?

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.990
And is that, do you ever think

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.640
of that pressure or you just put it out?

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.700
- I felt a lot of pressure after the tour

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.030
for Jagged Little Pill finished

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.293
and it was time to write the followup.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.020
And I remember going
to a studio in Canada,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.840
with my friend, Tim, when
I was under the auspices of

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.370
starting to write again and I
really didn't want to write,

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.600
it was that pressure buckle thing.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:03.433
- Yeah.

00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:04.890
- And I turned to him and I said,

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.160
I really don't want to write.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.840
And he said, he was the only person,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.470
'cause everyone else said, but your fans,

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.430
or you're gonna let everybody down,

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:12.677
everyone had an answer that was gonna

00:02:12.677 --> 00:02:15.460
shame my needing some rest.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.770
But then my friend, Tim, he
just turned to me and he said,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.918
well, let's just go get a sandwich.

00:02:19.918 --> 00:02:22.010
(Kelly laughing hysterically)

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:23.680
It was the first time that the pressure

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.164
had been completely taken off.

00:02:25.164 --> 00:02:29.010
We went and watched a movie,
I think, grab some food.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.610
And then when I came
back, I wrote, of course,

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.320
the law is such that I
wrote the first song for

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.420
Supposed Former Infatuation Junkie.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.873
- Oh my God, which is another great album!

00:02:36.873 --> 00:02:38.382
- (giggles) Thank you.

00:02:38.382 --> 00:02:40.720
- That was so great.
- Thank you.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.560
The pressure, the pressure, you know?

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:43.980
I'm assuming you can relate?

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.580
Like every grocery store, when's
the next record coming up?

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.900
When's the next record coming up?

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.240
- It's art, so you kinda have to wait

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.660
until you're inspired, right?

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.190
- Right, the receptivity has to,

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:57.210
it's almost like I show
up, this is the filter.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.580
So it siphons through my intellect.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:02.900
But the main part is me
being receptive and more yin.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.850
And most of my life, I'm such an alpha.

00:03:05.815 --> 00:03:07.890
And I'm in go, kind of lean forward mode.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.070
When it comes to writing,

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.033
I have to sit back and just receive.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:13.290
But then make sure I get it on tape.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.500
It takes however, lifetime
of living it and then for us,

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.060
or for me, it takes 10 minutes
to write it or 15 minutes.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.880
But whole lifetime of living it.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.910
- Her new album is called
Such Pretty Forks in the Road.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:26.054
Great title!

00:03:26.054 --> 00:03:29.240
This new album is very
personal, you say, right?

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.960
You get into depression, hard
emotions, hard conversations.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:36.190
Did you have second thoughts
about releasing that?

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:39.380
I know that you've always
been a very open writer,

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:40.213
especially in this time

00:03:40.213 --> 00:03:41.365
when everybody's kind of going through it,

00:03:41.365 --> 00:03:42.820
did you have second
thoughts about releasing it?

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.870
- No, actually I used to be terrified.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:46.870
And the night before any release.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.980
And then once it's out,
time and time again,

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:51.260
it just became so normal to share

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:54.573
autobiographically and in a raw way.

00:03:55.654 --> 00:03:56.850
Now I'm a lot less afraid,

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.870
and a lot of people have
intimated that it takes so much

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.570
courage to write the way that I write.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:04.980
But for me it takes courage
to talk with people directly

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.820
and resolve conflict and
bring up something tough

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.930
or stand up for myself
or, whereas in a song,

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.650
I'm writing that alone in a room,

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.560
no one's watching me, no one cares.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:16.870
I used to think I could get away with

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.660
just writing about it
and then running away

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.330
and not having to deal
with human beings (laughs).

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:22.840
But apparently that doesn't work.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:24.260
- It's hard though, it's hard!

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.170
Especially when you have a
lifetime of I'm imagining,

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.810
you're saying, in person
it's a different thing.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:32.600
That's probably took a while
to become the bold person

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.350
and in person, not just in song, right?

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:37.060
- Right, people would hear the songs

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:38.820
and they'd imagine they
were gonna meet someone

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.520
at the radio station or wherever it was.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.420
And I would walk in and
whatever I was being that day,

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.490
I might've been meek or
quiet or more contemplative.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.460
And I think their perception
was that I was gonna come in

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:50.410
and just like rip everything down

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.560
and spray paint the
walls and just (yells).

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.366
These days (chuckles), depends
when you catch me at home.

00:04:57.366 --> 00:04:58.490
(Kelly giggling)

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:00.500
I think my main goal
over the last many years

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.040
has been to take the clarity
and the empowered communication

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.140
that it takes to write a
song and actually apply it

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:09.600
while sitting across from someone,

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.220
where the fight, flight, freeze kicks in,

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.053
when we're in conflict,

00:05:13.053 --> 00:05:15.150
but it's not kicking
in when we're writing.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:16.770
When we're writing, there's this lucidity

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.160
and this flow and it feels like--

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:18.993
- And there's an openness

00:05:18.993 --> 00:05:20.380
'cause you're alone in your own moment.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:21.680
And sometimes I feel like artists,

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.250
we're always surrounded by people

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.597
and sometimes we do
that and we choose that

00:05:25.597 --> 00:05:27.250
and sometimes it just happens.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:29.470
And so sometimes it's you're only solace,

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:31.423
it's your only place to go.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.340
On your new album, there's
a song called Smiling,

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.737
and I love it so much!

00:05:38.737 --> 00:05:41.940
It's because it's not, I know
a lot of people will say,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.000
oh, that's for people in
the limelight, it's not.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.140
That's for people in any vocation.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.741
Any part of your life, you
can be having that moment

00:05:49.741 --> 00:05:52.970
where it's like, I'm
going through so much,

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:54.330
but I guess I'll keep smiling

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.010
since that's what everyone's expecting.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:59.630
And I'm supposed to be fine.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.412
- Especially as women too, right?

00:06:01.412 --> 00:06:04.500
We're sort of, archetypally,
it's almost like a

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:09.230
demand to be that hearth
overseeing, nurturing,

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:10.950
just normal for us in our bodies,

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.690
but we're also applying ourselves

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:14.410
in this whole yang world too.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:17.680
So for me it's all your
different archetypes

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.090
that I love being the
teacher, the mom, the artist,

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:24.630
the activist, the
outspoken, the channeler,

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.070
all these different
archetypes and just the hats.

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.890
I'm talking to you, you of all people know

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.023
the hats of mom and then producer.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.350
Just going through all
these different roles

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:40.840
and because of the sensitivity
and the empath qualities,

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:42.240
I'm just so much more responsible

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:43.580
for making sure I can take breaks.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.853
Even if it's hiding in a bathroom.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.440
Carrying ear plugs with
me, don't tell anybody.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:50.273
(Alanis chuckling)

00:06:50.273 --> 00:06:51.106
- No, no.

00:06:51.106 --> 00:06:52.803
- I'm wearing them right
now, no, just kidding.

00:06:52.803 --> 00:06:54.853
(women laughing)

00:06:54.853 --> 00:06:56.070
I'm reading your lips.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:58.373
- I'm actually not listening to you.

00:06:58.373 --> 00:06:59.824
- I can't hear a word
you're saying, Kelly.

00:06:59.824 --> 00:07:02.473
- (giggles) No, that's so true.

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.610
I've had this conversation
with someone very close to me

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:09.070
and I'm like, I'm not trying to be rude,

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:11.920
when I pull out the
ear buds and the music,

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:13.832
sometimes I'm not even playing anything.

00:07:13.832 --> 00:07:14.665
- Right.

00:07:14.665 --> 00:07:17.420
- I just want silence, I just
want a moment to where I'm not

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:20.170
being talked at, or I don't
feel like I'm having to engage.

00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:23.360
'Cause I feel like not
just our jobs, all jobs,

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:24.310
I feel like we're a society--

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:29.260
- Everybody, a society
of on-ness, like the off.

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:30.320
I mean our nervous systems

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.390
were not meant to be
in cortisol mode 24/7.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.580
And we're actually chronic stress

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.195
in the way that we're all experiencing.

00:07:37.195 --> 00:07:39.410
- Fight or flight, yeah.
- Is traumatic.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:40.883
- It's constant, I know.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:45.750
Anyway, I'm such a fan and I did find out,

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:48.670
somebody told me that you
had been to one of my shows

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:51.000
and I'm going to tell you if that's true,

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.870
I am so glad that no one
told me you were there,

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:59.084
because I would have
(beep) a Christmas turkey.

00:07:59.084 --> 00:08:01.320
(women chuckling)

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.470
- When people come to my
shows whom I adore, I'm like,

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:05.930
you specifically can't
tell me because that's all

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:07.817
I'm gonna think about when
I'm on stage (giggles).

00:08:07.817 --> 00:08:09.917
- Oh, no one ever told me, not even after.

00:08:11.907 --> 00:08:14.330
There's a strict policy on
my door, I don't want to know

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:18.110
if anyone in the audience
is, I don't care how famous,

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:18.970
I don't want to know.

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:21.660
'Cause it makes me nervous
and I don't get nervous.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:22.493
- When you're singing,

00:08:22.493 --> 00:08:24.088
are you thinking of that
person the whole time?

00:08:24.088 --> 00:08:24.940
- That's what I'm saying!

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:26.560
- I did the musical for Jagged Little Pill

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:29.770
and a bunch of the
beautiful, beautiful singers,

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:32.620
performers, portrayers,
came to see my shows.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:34.050
And unfortunately I'd find out

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:36.290
that they were in the
audience before I was.

00:08:36.290 --> 00:08:39.137
And their (laughs) the
most beautiful singers.

00:08:39.137 --> 00:08:40.381
- That's so much pressure!

00:08:40.381 --> 00:08:41.520
- And I was just like, oh God.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:45.370
Now I have to watch my pitch
and hit all the right notes

00:08:46.235 --> 00:08:47.480
and it was horrifying.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:50.210
I learned my lesson probably
the more challenging way,

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:53.650
which is my whole show
gets kerfuffled if I know

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:56.390
someone's in the audience
who's beautiful art I love.

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:57.516
- How is that though?

00:08:57.516 --> 00:09:02.516
You have such a great
catalog and you were such,

00:09:02.602 --> 00:09:07.103
just a pop culture icon, that
they did a whole musical.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.730
Was that crazy?

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.860
- It was the first amount of objectivity

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:13.840
I've ever had on the
songs, which was cool.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:15.270
And then also collaboratively.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:17.874
I've always felt that
theater was this nomadic,

00:09:17.874 --> 00:09:22.250
communal village thing,
that as a solo artist,

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:25.330
it got pretty lonely on the
road, just as a solo artist,

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:26.755
doing everything kind of alone.

00:09:26.755 --> 00:09:29.370
And I know that bands and
the Beatles and everyone

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.640
had their own challenges
for being a group, I get it.

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.260
But being alone was a rough one.

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:36.730
- It's so hard.
- Being in a community,

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:40.810
of people in the musical and
Diablo Cody pulled all the

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:42.760
characters out of the songs themselves,

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:44.650
so there's this real kind of integration

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.290
between the music and the story.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:49.630
And some people were afraid
of some of the content

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:52.160
and I just called them
and I said, I got this.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.000
I will back anything up that
we bring up that is scary.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.000
And lord knows Diane, policy
director, did tons of research

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.550
to be respectful in each
category of challenge.

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:06.410
They did so much research and
I did a little bit myself too.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:10.020
- But I think it would be upsetting

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:14.610
to see something with your
inspiration, your work,

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:17.990
and not see something
raw and real and intimate

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:21.320
because it would be so opposite of you.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:23.763
It seems like at least,
musically speaking.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.690
I don't know, I just think
that you've achieved a whole

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:28.570
'nother level and they're like,

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:30.970
we want to make all a
whole show about (laughs).

00:10:32.194 --> 00:10:33.160
- Thank God it wasn't about me.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:36.270
I'll do a one person play at
some point or a musical play.

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:38.100
I have little flashes
of it, do you have that?

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.690
Where for a long time, I just have images

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:42.870
or pictures of where I was going next.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:45.050
And then sometimes it's fully blank screen

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:46.786
and I'm like, uh oh.

00:10:46.786 --> 00:10:48.360
(Kelly chuckling)

